WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-CA

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.480
Your ideas sound brilliant in your head,&nbsp;
but do they confuse readers when you write&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.480 --> 00:00:08.720
them down? Well, after 10 years as an&nbsp;
English professor and writing coach,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.720 --> 00:00:13.600
I've found that even the simplest&nbsp;
ideas can be made to sound complex,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.600 --> 00:00:19.680
and even the most complex ideas can be made&nbsp;
to sound simple. It's not actually your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:25.200
ideas that confuse readers. It all comes&nbsp;
down to the way that you structure them.

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:29.600
So in this video, I'm going to show you the&nbsp;
coffee stain problem, which is why readers can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:35.840
get so confused by your ideas even when they&nbsp;
ring clear in your own mind. Then I'm going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.840 --> 00:00:42.240
to give you the experience of readers as they&nbsp;
work their way through your text. And finally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:48.240
I'm going to show you three types of structure&nbsp;
in your writing: one that you're already using,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:52.800
which you should probably stop using,&nbsp;
and two that you can start using today.

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:58.160
But first, why is it that we writers&nbsp;
fail to recognize the patterns in our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.160 --> 00:01:03.680
own writing that cause difficulty with&nbsp;
readers? Well, think about what happens&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.680 --> 00:01:09.200
whenever you're trying to diagnose and fix&nbsp;
problems in your own writing. You've written&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:14.240
a draft and now you're looking it over.&nbsp;
Or maybe you have given it to a reader,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:18.320
you've got some feedback, and now you're&nbsp;
going through giving some corrections to it.

00:01:18.320 --> 00:01:23.440
And so what do you do here? Well, here's what you&nbsp;
think happens. All right. You take your draft,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.440 --> 00:01:28.800
and your draft has all these words on it. And&nbsp;
so you look at those words, and you know the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:33.920
meaning of those words, and you're the one&nbsp;
who arranged those words in that order. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.920 --> 00:01:39.440
so those words in that order give you a certain&nbsp;
meaning. And then you check. You say, "All right,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.440 --> 00:01:47.680
does this meaning that I interpret in this text&nbsp;
correspond to the meaning that I intend — the one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.680 --> 00:01:52.960
that I want to express?" And you say, "Aha, yes,&nbsp;
perfect. That's exactly what I want to express."

00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:58.720
That's what many of us think is happening in the&nbsp;
proofreading and revision process. But what's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.720 --> 00:02:04.080
really happening here is what's called the coffee&nbsp;
stain problem. And here's why it fails readers.

00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:07.680
So imagine this. You wake up one morning&nbsp;
and you're getting ready to write,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:12.160
and you pour yourself a cup of coffee,&nbsp;
when suddenly a great idea strikes you.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.160 --> 00:02:16.240
And so you rush to your desk. You set down&nbsp;
your coffee cup. You reach for your pen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.240 --> 00:02:20.320
and in all your excitement to start&nbsp;
writing, you knock over your coffee&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.320 --> 00:02:25.120
cup. And so now coffee is staining the&nbsp;
paper that you're going to write on.

00:02:25.120 --> 00:02:31.280
So although this paper has no words on it, that&nbsp;
stain — that particular shape, and the depth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.280 --> 00:02:36.320
and the color, and everything — it will still&nbsp;
serve to remind you of the idea that you had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.320 --> 00:02:43.280
when you made that stain. The problem here is&nbsp;
when you hand this stained paper off to readers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.280 --> 00:02:48.240
that stain would mean nothing to a reader,&nbsp;
because they weren't there in the room with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.240 --> 00:02:54.000
you when you had the idea. All they have is&nbsp;
a blank sheet of paper with a certain shape&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:59.680
and pattern of your coffee stain on it. In&nbsp;
other words, those patterns mean nothing to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.680 --> 00:03:07.200
your reader. They can't use it to reconstruct the&nbsp;
idea that you had whenever you created that stain.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:11.600
The stain will always remind you&nbsp;
of the brilliant idea that you had,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.600 --> 00:03:17.440
but it will never remind readers, because they&nbsp;
weren't there with you when you had the idea.

00:03:17.440 --> 00:03:21.840
So the point of this thought experiment&nbsp;
is really just to show the difficulty of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:27.920
editing our own work. Like, when you look at&nbsp;
your own text, you're not actually reading it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.920 --> 00:03:33.200
You're just remembering it. When you wrote&nbsp;
the text, you had an idea in your mind,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.200 --> 00:03:40.400
and then you chose a series of words in a&nbsp;
particular order in order to convey that idea.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.400 --> 00:03:47.120
So whenever you look at those words in that order&nbsp;
again, what you're trying to do is see whether&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.120 --> 00:03:52.320
those words correspond to the idea that you had.&nbsp;
And of course they do, because you're the one who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.320 --> 00:03:58.480
chose the words. So in other words, what we call&nbsp;
proofreading and editing is not really reading&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.480 --> 00:04:05.200
and interpretation. It's more about association&nbsp;
and remembering the ideas that you already had.

00:04:05.200 --> 00:04:11.120
But if you can never stand outside yourself to&nbsp;
get beyond your own associations and experience&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.120 --> 00:04:16.880
the text as your reader will, then how can&nbsp;
we ever edit our own work? How can you ever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.880 --> 00:04:21.120
predict whether a particular piece of&nbsp;
writing is going to work for readers?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.120 --> 00:04:24.560
Well, what you need to do is&nbsp;
learn the structures and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.560 --> 00:04:30.480
structural locations where readers expect&nbsp;
to find particular pieces of information.

00:04:30.480 --> 00:04:32.720
And you can experience this right now. So let me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.720 --> 00:04:37.280
show you how this works. I'm going&nbsp;
to read a series of numbers for you,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.280 --> 00:04:44.560
and what I'd like you to do is just predict&nbsp;
the next number in the series. That's it.

00:04:44.560 --> 00:04:48.160
Now, I know, I know I'm breaking the fourth&nbsp;
wall here, and I'm actually reaching out and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.160 --> 00:04:53.600
asking you to do something, but I promise it'll&nbsp;
be worth it. So I'm going to read you six numbers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.600 --> 00:04:57.040
and I'll give you a little pause, but I&nbsp;
recommend pausing the screen if you need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.040 --> 00:05:01.760
another chance. And what I'd like you to do&nbsp;
is just predict the next number in the series.

00:05:01.760 --> 00:05:16.400
All right. So here's the first&nbsp;
series. 2, 3, 5, 7, 11, 13.

00:05:16.400 --> 00:05:20.000
The next number in the series is 17.

00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:35.440
All right, here's the next series. Predict&nbsp;
the last number. 2, 3, 5, 8, 13, 21.

00:05:35.440 --> 00:05:39.520
The next number in the series is 34.

00:05:39.520 --> 00:05:53.520
All right, last series. 2, 3, 5, 9, 4, 3.

00:05:53.520 --> 00:05:57.840
The last number in the series is 2.

00:05:57.840 --> 00:06:01.440
Now, in case you guessed any of those wrong&nbsp;
and you're looking for an explanation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.440 --> 00:06:10.640
the first series was a series of prime&nbsp;
numbers. So that's 2, 3, 5, 7, 11, 13,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.640 --> 00:06:16.560
and the next prime number in that series is&nbsp;
17. The second set of numbers comes from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.560 --> 00:06:22.000
Fibonacc— Fibon— Fibonacci, Fibonacci, Fibonacci.&nbsp;
I've never actually heard that word said, but I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.000 --> 00:06:26.480
think it's pronounced Fibonacci. The Fibonacci&nbsp;
series. So you take the two and the three and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.480 --> 00:06:31.440
you add them together and you get a five. And&nbsp;
then you take the three and the five together and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.440 --> 00:06:38.960
you add them together and you get an eight, so on&nbsp;
and so forth. And then it would be 13 + 21 is 34.

00:06:38.960 --> 00:06:42.720
And now for that final series of numbers —&nbsp;
did you catch the last number? Once again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.720 --> 00:06:49.760
it was 2, 3, 5, 9, 4, 3, 2. So&nbsp;
did you guess the two? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.760 --> 00:06:55.280
if you did, then congratulations, because&nbsp;
you just guessed my mother's phone number.

00:06:55.280 --> 00:07:01.280
But if that was your mother's phone number, then&nbsp;
the series would seem entirely coherent to you.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.280 --> 00:07:07.120
But to anybody from outside your head,&nbsp;
it's going to seem completely opaque. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.120 --> 00:07:09.360
they're not just going to throw&nbsp;
up their hands and say, "Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.360 --> 00:07:13.440
I guess there's no pattern here." What&nbsp;
they're going to do is search for one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.440 --> 00:07:19.120
and often provide one that you didn't intend.&nbsp;
When you write the way that you think, you are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.120 --> 00:07:26.240
writing according to that third sequence, which&nbsp;
is entirely coherent to you but opaque to readers.

00:07:26.240 --> 00:07:30.400
So how can you make your writing more&nbsp;
coherent to readers? Is there some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.400 --> 00:07:35.120
universal structure that we can use, some&nbsp;
master prompt that we can give to AI? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.120 --> 00:07:38.480
unfortunately not, because that's&nbsp;
not really how writing works.

00:07:38.480 --> 00:07:44.240
And I know this firsthand, because recently&nbsp;
I went back and reread my master's thesis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.240 --> 00:07:50.640
which I haven't looked at for about 10 years.&nbsp;
And at the time, I thought this was the greatest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.640 --> 00:07:56.640
thing that I had ever produced. It's something&nbsp;
I worked on endlessly. I would wake up early&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.640 --> 00:08:01.840
before class and work on it, and I'd be working&nbsp;
on it all night, and it was one of these projects&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.840 --> 00:08:05.440
where you forget to eat — you're working on&nbsp;
it so much. So I was really absorbed in this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.440 --> 00:08:11.520
and by the time I handed it in, it was immaculate.&nbsp;
It was the most perfect thing I had ever written.

00:08:11.520 --> 00:08:15.520
And of course, when I look it over now,&nbsp;
10 years later, quite embarrassed by it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.520 --> 00:08:21.680
Not exactly because of the ideas, but actually&nbsp;
because of the way that the ideas are structured.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.680 --> 00:08:27.440
I thought things were structured so clearly.&nbsp;
I thought I had just nailed the organization.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.440 --> 00:08:32.160
But I look over it now, and there are parts of&nbsp;
it that are honestly incomprehensible to me.

00:08:32.160 --> 00:08:38.720
But isn't that interesting? Because if coherence&nbsp;
is part of the text, then surely when I look over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.720 --> 00:08:43.200
this text, I would be like, "Well, there's&nbsp;
the coherence," right? It was coherent when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.200 --> 00:08:49.920
I wrote it 10 years ago. So why is it incoherent&nbsp;
now? It's the same text, but there's essentially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.920 --> 00:08:54.000
a different reader, because I've become a&nbsp;
different person. I've changed in that time.

00:08:54.000 --> 00:08:59.360
In other words, coherence is not&nbsp;
a property of the text. Like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.360 --> 00:09:05.520
we have this idea — which is wrong, but we have&nbsp;
this idea — that, you know, here's the writer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.520 --> 00:09:14.720
and the writer is thinking and transcribing&nbsp;
that idea into text, and then that text gets&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.720 --> 00:09:21.840
handed off to readers. And so if the writer&nbsp;
can make this text coherent and clear, then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.840 --> 00:09:27.600
the reader will look at that text and they will&nbsp;
perceive that coherence. And if that was true,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.600 --> 00:09:33.200
then you would be able to automate this to AI,&nbsp;
and you would be able to just read over your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.200 --> 00:09:40.720
text and fix it, to apply some templates or some&nbsp;
very kind of fixed patterns and make it coherent.

00:09:40.720 --> 00:09:44.960
However, what the experience that I&nbsp;
had with my master's thesis shows is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.960 --> 00:09:50.240
that coherence doesn't really work this&nbsp;
way. It's more like this, where the idea&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.240 --> 00:09:56.880
emerges through the relationship between the&nbsp;
writer, the reader, and the text. All these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.880 --> 00:10:03.920
three elements are in a kind of relationship&nbsp;
with each other, and the idea emerges from it.

00:10:03.920 --> 00:10:09.360
And so when we talk about coherence, what&nbsp;
we're talking about is structure. And that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.360 --> 00:10:17.920
structure doesn't exist inside the text. It's not&nbsp;
in here. It's in the reader. And so the question&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.920 --> 00:10:24.800
of coherence is a question of: how can we get&nbsp;
this reader to perceive structure in the text?

00:10:24.800 --> 00:10:29.360
And you've already had the experience&nbsp;
of perceiving three different kinds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.360 --> 00:10:32.480
of structure. So what are those three kinds?

00:10:32.480 --> 00:10:37.680
Well, the last kind that you experienced, with&nbsp;
my mother's phone number, was an experience of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.680 --> 00:10:42.720
private structure. You didn't have&nbsp;
any reference for the structure that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.720 --> 00:10:49.360
was organizing those three elements, and so&nbsp;
you provided one yourself. Maybe you took a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.360 --> 00:10:54.080
guess. Maybe you just got frustrated and threw&nbsp;
your hands up and said there is no structure.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.080 --> 00:11:00.160
But you couldn't perceive the structure, and&nbsp;
so the kind of structure-searching mechanism&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.160 --> 00:11:05.280
in your brain posited one. And that's&nbsp;
essentially the coffee stain problem.

00:11:05.280 --> 00:11:10.320
Now, more practically, in the context of writing,&nbsp;
that kind of structure — which is clear to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.320 --> 00:11:16.400
writer and opaque to the reader — is what results&nbsp;
in a lot of metadiscourse. And that's why a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.400 --> 00:11:23.360
of academic prose, and just really a lot of,&nbsp;
like, bad writing, has a lot of these signposts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.360 --> 00:11:29.280
where writers are kind of doing two jobs at&nbsp;
once. They're both expressing themselves and then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.280 --> 00:11:37.280
filling in all the logical gaps and explaining&nbsp;
to the reader how one idea follows from another.

00:11:37.280 --> 00:11:42.560
And generally readers don't like this. It's not&nbsp;
to say you can never do this. This is writing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.560 --> 00:11:48.560
You can do whatever you want. But just understand&nbsp;
that whenever your text is organized according to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.560 --> 00:11:55.200
your own associations, in the way that makes sense&nbsp;
to you, with a disregard of the reader, you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.200 --> 00:12:00.320
either going to abandon the reader and they're&nbsp;
not going to be able to use your text, or, if you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:00.320 --> 00:12:06.480
want them to use your text, then you're going to&nbsp;
have to fill in all the associations and logical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.480 --> 00:12:12.800
connections that are apparent to you but opaque&nbsp;
to them. So it's not really a good way to go.

00:12:12.800 --> 00:12:16.000
But now, the second kind of structure&nbsp;
that you experienced was represented&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.000 --> 00:12:20.960
by the prime numbers, and that's what&nbsp;
we would call a contextual structure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:20.960 --> 00:12:27.520
because it drew on your background information.&nbsp;
You came to the text knowing some basic things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:27.520 --> 00:12:32.720
about math. And whenever you looked at the text&nbsp;
and you saw those numbers in that sequence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:32.720 --> 00:12:38.240
you recognized it — drawing on your background&nbsp;
information — from a certain kind of pattern.

00:12:38.240 --> 00:12:44.640
And that, more practically in writing, would be&nbsp;
represented by something like genre awareness.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:44.640 --> 00:12:49.920
So all readers in nonfiction are going to&nbsp;
expect an introduction followed by some body&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:49.920 --> 00:12:53.680
paragraphs. If you're writing a scientific&nbsp;
report, then you know that your readers are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:53.680 --> 00:12:58.160
going to expect an introduction, followed&nbsp;
by the methods, followed by the findings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:58.160 --> 00:13:02.800
followed by the discussion. And that's not&nbsp;
given to them in the text. They're just drawing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:02.800 --> 00:13:08.720
on their background information from seeing&nbsp;
different versions of this kind of genre before.

00:13:08.720 --> 00:13:12.960
But now, the third kind of structure that you&nbsp;
experienced was represented by the Fibonacci&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:12.960 --> 00:13:19.680
sequence. And you perceived that structure&nbsp;
not from your own background knowledge. Like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:19.680 --> 00:13:23.920
you might have not even known that&nbsp;
it was called the Fibonacci sequence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:23.920 --> 00:13:28.880
and you certainly hadn't memorized all&nbsp;
the numbers in that sequence. You just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:28.880 --> 00:13:34.320
looked at the sequence itself, and the&nbsp;
pattern kind of emerged from the text.

00:13:34.320 --> 00:13:40.960
And in your own writing, that's what we would call&nbsp;
a textual pattern. It's not one that the reader&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:40.960 --> 00:13:46.960
requires any background knowledge to recognize.&nbsp;
It's one that's provided to them from the text.

00:13:46.960 --> 00:13:52.000
And if you want a visual guide to four expert&nbsp;
patterns that readers will easily recognize&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:52.000 --> 00:13:56.480
in your text, you can get my Visual Guide&nbsp;
to Expert Writing. It's four pages long,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:56.480 --> 00:14:02.720
and on this page I have four examples of textual&nbsp;
patterns with model texts taken from professional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:02.720 --> 00:14:06.960
prose. So you can grab that down below the&nbsp;
like button, or you can watch this video next,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:06.960 --> 00:14:24.400
because it's a deep dive into those four&nbsp;
textual patterns. So be sure to go check it out.

